course: dictionary
lesson: 4205
title: Soft words with a story
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Gosh!» is a small word people say to show a feeling: surprise, shock or delight. It can also add emphasis, as in «Gosh, it's hot», where it makes the heat sound stronger.
explain: Long ago, people did not want to say God's name in anger, so they bent the words into softer shapes. God became «gosh» and «golly», so «gosh» is a mild stand-in for «God» and is polite to say.
explain: Read the clues around a word. «So when a grandmother drops a cup and cries "Gosh darn it!"...» She sounds cross, but the words are polite, and nobody minds. Ask yourself: is the word naming a thing, or showing a feeling? Gosh shows a feeling.
fact: Gosh is an exclamation: a word people say to show a feeling.
fact: «Gosh» means an exclamation of surprise, shock or delight.
fact: «Gosh» is a mild stand-in for «God», so it is a soft, family-friendly word.
fact: Other soft stand-ins include «gee», «golly», «jeepers», «cripes» and «crikey».
fact: «Gosh darn it» is an American polite form of «God damn it».
exercise: In «Gosh, it's hot», what is gosh doing? | Saying goodbye | *Adding emphasis, to make the heat sound stronger | Asking a question | Naming the heat
exercise: Why did people bend God into «gosh» long ago? | *They did not want to say God's name in anger | They wanted a new word for hot | They were making a joke about church | They needed a word for surprise in books
exercise: Which word is a mild stand-in for «Christ»? | Golly | Gosh | Darn | *Crikey
test:
«Gosh!» is said when someone is surprised. What feeling does it show? | Boredom | Sleepiness | *Surprise | Anger
test:
A grandmother cries «Gosh darn it!» after dropping a cup. Why does nobody mind? | *It is a polite, softened form, so it is not rude | It is a real prayer that everyone must say | It is a shout that means the cup is broken | It is a word only children use
exam: In «Gosh, it's hot», the word gosh mostly does what job? | Names a place | *Adds emphasis to a feeling | Joins two sentences | Gives a question its answer
